# A comprehension that builds verdicts. The list works on both backends now (roadmap Gap R.112,
# ADR 0259); the set and the dict do not, because their compile-time fold writes a static global and a
# static global has no tag table to write True into — the fold now declines rather than store the
# number and let the printer follow it (roadmap Gap R.116).
#
# What each engine answers today:
#   --interp  [True, 1, 1]  {True}  {1: True}  {True: 1}
#   --aot     refuses, in words, exit 1: "a comprehension of verdicts needs the tagged set builder,
#             which a set comprehension does not have yet" — no line is printed at all
#   python3   [True, 1, 1]  {True}  {1: True}  {True: 1}
#
# The list row is here so the family stays one row: it is the shape that IS built tagged, and the
# refusal above is only believable next to it.
print([x for x in [True, 1, 1]])
print({True for x in [1]})
print({1: True for x in [1]})
print({True: 1 for x in [1]})
